1
00:00:02,185 --> 00:00:04,546
Now we'll start with the bubble sort

2
00:00:04,996 --> 00:00:07,599
algorithm, which is, I would say, the

3
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:10,475
easiest and the simplest sorting algorithm

4
00:00:10,475 --> 00:00:11,278
you can come up with.

5
00:00:12,162 --> 00:00:14,717
Now here we have an unsorted list, and we

6
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:17,577
would sort this in ascending or descending

7
00:00:17,705 --> 00:00:19,874
order with the bubble sort algorithm

8
00:00:20,517 --> 00:00:23,200
by comparing 2 items at a time

9
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:26,269
and then by, well, sorting these 2 items.

10
00:00:26,542 --> 00:00:28,529
And we would go through the entire array

11
00:00:28,529 --> 00:00:29,443
multiple times,

12
00:00:29,972 --> 00:00:32,488
every time comparing 2 items at a time for

13
00:00:32,488 --> 00:00:35,052
the entire array until all pairs were

14
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:36,286
compared and sorted.

15
00:00:36,959 --> 00:00:39,203
So let's now have a look at that in theory.

16
00:00:39,844 --> 00:00:43,114
We would start with the first 2 items in the

17
00:00:43,114 --> 00:00:46,255
array, so with item 1, the item at index 0,

18
00:00:46,576 --> 00:00:48,868
and item 2, the item at index 1,

19
00:00:49,605 --> 00:00:51,833
and we would compare those items and

20
00:00:51,865 --> 00:00:53,596
depending on whether we wanna sort in

21
00:00:53,852 --> 00:00:56,369
ascending or descending order, we would

22
00:00:56,417 --> 00:00:59,932
check if item 1, the 3 in this case, is

23
00:00:59,965 --> 00:01:03,063
greater or smaller than item 2, the 10 in

24
00:01:03,095 --> 00:01:05,374
this case. Now let's say we wanna sort in

25
00:01:05,647 --> 00:01:08,231
ascending order, so we would check if the

26
00:01:08,231 --> 00:01:09,868
first item, 3,

27
00:01:10,462 --> 00:01:13,239
is bigger than the second item.

28
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:15,711
If that would be the case, we would need to

29
00:01:15,711 --> 00:01:18,793
swap those 2 items to, well, put them in

30
00:01:18,890 --> 00:01:20,976
ascending order. Here, of course, that

31
00:01:20,976 --> 00:01:23,223
condition would not be met because these 2

32
00:01:23,304 --> 00:01:26,637
items already are sorted correctly. They are

33
00:01:26,846 --> 00:01:30,275
in ascending order. So we would move on and

34
00:01:30,291 --> 00:01:33,271
compare the first item to the third item,

35
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:34,745
-3 in this case.

36
00:01:35,578 --> 00:01:37,020
Now we apply the same check.

37
00:01:37,565 --> 00:01:40,930
Is that first item greater than that third

38
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:43,798
item? If yes, we need to swap them because

39
00:01:44,214 --> 00:01:47,178
these 2 items then certainly are in the

40
00:01:47,194 --> 00:01:49,742
wrong order. And for that, it does not

41
00:01:49,742 --> 00:01:51,969
matter that there's an item between them,

42
00:01:52,322 --> 00:01:52,514
10,

43
00:01:53,044 --> 00:01:55,340
which might be even bigger than any of those

44
00:01:55,372 --> 00:01:57,603
2 items. We'll take care about that in the

45
00:01:57,603 --> 00:02:00,156
next iteration. As I said, we're going to

46
00:02:00,172 --> 00:02:02,693
compare all pairs and we're going to do that

47
00:02:02,773 --> 00:02:03,608
multiple times.

48
00:02:04,426 --> 00:02:07,717
So here we're looking at 3 and -3, and we

49
00:02:07,734 --> 00:02:11,185
would of course find out that -3 is smaller

50
00:02:11,185 --> 00:02:14,091
than 3, so we need to swap those 2 items.

51
00:02:14,316 --> 00:02:16,820
And that is what we would do, we would swap

52
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:18,731
those 2 items in our array.

53
00:02:19,757 --> 00:02:22,482
And now important, we keep comparing the

54
00:02:22,578 --> 00:02:26,345
first item to the next item. So we're not

55
00:02:26,345 --> 00:02:30,032
going to compare 3 to 48 now, but instead -3

56
00:02:30,032 --> 00:02:33,110
to 48. So we stick to the first item, and it

57
00:02:33,158 --> 00:02:35,771
doesn't matter that the first item changed.

58
00:02:36,027 --> 00:02:39,153
We focus on the first item by index, not

59
00:02:39,153 --> 00:02:41,141
what was previously the first item.

60
00:02:41,991 --> 00:02:45,004
So we compare our new first item to the next

61
00:02:45,004 --> 00:02:47,601
item in line, which is 48. We check if our

62
00:02:47,665 --> 00:02:49,813
first item is greater than that and if we

63
00:02:49,813 --> 00:02:52,044
therefore need to make a swap. But that's of

64
00:02:52,076 --> 00:02:54,228
course not the case here, so we keep it the

65
00:02:54,228 --> 00:02:57,150
way it is. We move on to the next item, no

66
00:02:57,295 --> 00:03:00,041
swap required here either. So we move on to

67
00:03:00,041 --> 00:03:02,530
the next item, no swap required,

68
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:05,517
and also not for the last item. So now we're

69
00:03:05,565 --> 00:03:08,760
done comparing the first item to all our

70
00:03:08,841 --> 00:03:10,976
following items for the first time.

71
00:03:12,068 --> 00:03:14,734
Now we move on to the second item in the

72
00:03:14,734 --> 00:03:17,078
array, and we again compare that to all

73
00:03:17,351 --> 00:03:20,322
items after it. And that's important only to

74
00:03:20,322 --> 00:03:23,211
the items after it because we know that the

75
00:03:23,211 --> 00:03:25,523
items in front of it have to be smaller

76
00:03:25,619 --> 00:03:25,956
already.

77
00:03:26,663 --> 00:03:30,146
So we compare 10 to 3 and we make that check

78
00:03:30,146 --> 00:03:32,265
if 10 is greater than 3, which of course is

79
00:03:32,313 --> 00:03:35,074
the case. So we need to swap those 2 values.

80
00:03:35,668 --> 00:03:38,719
Now again, we stick to the second item. The

81
00:03:38,719 --> 00:03:41,335
fact that it's swapped does not matter. So

82
00:03:41,351 --> 00:03:44,096
we're going to compare this new second item

83
00:03:44,096 --> 00:03:46,906
to the next item in line. The 48, and we

84
00:03:46,906 --> 00:03:50,423
check if 3 is greater than 48, in which case

85
00:03:50,423 --> 00:03:52,960
we would need to swap those values. Now,

86
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:55,819
no swap is required here, so we move on and

87
00:03:55,819 --> 00:03:58,822
compare it to 5. No swap required. Compare

88
00:03:58,886 --> 00:04:01,889
it to 33. No swap required. Compare it to

89
00:04:01,889 --> 00:04:03,945
99. No swap required.

90
00:04:04,555 --> 00:04:06,418
And we move on to the next item in line.

91
00:04:06,563 --> 00:04:08,923
Compare that to 48. No swap required.

92
00:04:09,469 --> 00:04:12,537
Compare that to 5, and a swap is required.

93
00:04:12,858 --> 00:04:13,724
So we do that

94
00:04:14,270 --> 00:04:17,094
and we compare that new third value, the 5,

95
00:04:17,111 --> 00:04:19,710
to the next item in line. No swap required,

96
00:04:20,063 --> 00:04:23,096
no swap required for 99 either. So we move

97
00:04:23,096 --> 00:04:26,450
on to the next item. We compare that to 10

98
00:04:26,482 --> 00:04:27,092
in this case,

99
00:04:27,670 --> 00:04:29,708
and 10 of course is smaller, so we need to

100
00:04:29,708 --> 00:04:31,152
swap those 2 values.

101
00:04:31,682 --> 00:04:34,763
Now we compare 10 to 33. No swap required

102
00:04:34,827 --> 00:04:37,074
and no swap required either for 99.

103
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:40,508
We move on to the next pair. Here we do need

104
00:04:40,508 --> 00:04:41,696
to swap them, of course.

105
00:04:42,370 --> 00:04:43,445
And then we compare that

106
00:04:44,039 --> 00:04:46,334
and we move on to the last pair, compare

107
00:04:46,463 --> 00:04:49,881
those 2, no swap required, we got our final

108
00:04:50,058 --> 00:04:52,931
list. That's how the bubble sort algorithm

109
00:04:52,947 --> 00:04:53,268
works.

110
00:04:53,845 --> 00:04:56,237
We go through the list multiple times and we

111
00:04:56,413 --> 00:04:59,447
every time compare one item to all the other

112
00:04:59,607 --> 00:05:02,352
following items in the list. Now let's see

113
00:05:02,657 --> 00:05:05,048
how we could write this algorithm in code.
